-- title:
--   The centralizer of $\mathcal T$ is $\mathcal S\cup\mathcal L$, a non-abelian group (corrected: L = centralizer of T minus S)
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge1$. Let $C$ be the set of Pauli operators (modulo phase) that commute with every element of $\mathcal T$, and let $\mathcal L := C\setminus\mathcal S$. Then:
--
--   1. $C$ consists exactly of the $P(a,b)$ whose $X$-part $a$ is constant along every row and whose $Z$-part $b$ is constant along every column, i.e. the products of full $X$-rows and full $Z$-columns;
--   2. $C$ is a group: it contains the identity and is closed under products;
--   3. $\mathcal S\cup\mathcal L = C$, so $\mathcal L$ together with $\mathcal S$ forms a group, while $\mathcal L$ alone does not contain the identity;
--   4. $C$ is not abelian: it contains two anticommuting elements;
--   5. every element of $\mathcal L$ commutes with every element of $\mathcal T$ and of $\mathcal S$.
--
--   The elements of $\mathcal L$ are the logical operators of the encoded qubit, among them $\bar X$ and $\bar Z$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: lattice size n, rows and columns indexed 1..n, generators indexed by Z_{n−1} → n = m + 1 with m : ℕ; rows and columns Fin (m + 1), 0-based; generators indexed by Fin m acting on i.castSucc, i.succ; reason: Lean convention; n = 0 has no qubits, so every lattice of the paper is an instance
--   - encoding: Pauli operators with phases ±1, ±i (the (−1)^φ of (8), (13)) → pairs (a, b) of bit matrices modulo phase; multiplication is addition, commutation is the symplectic bit symp; reason: membership in T, S, C(S), commutation and weight do not depend on the phase
--   - reading: S as the set (10): ⊕_i(∧_j a_ij) = 0, ⊕_j(∧_i b_ij) = 0 → S = the group generated by S^X_i, S^Z_j of (11) (SGroup m); reason: (10) taken alone admits non-full rows (n = 2: a single X_{1,1} satisfies it but anticommutes with Z_{1,1}Z_{1,2} ∈ T, contrary to p.4); the prose before (10) and the generators (11) agree, and S_props proves that the span equals the prose set
--   - correction: L as printed in (13): both ⊕_i(∧_j a_ij) = 1 and ⊕_j(∧_i b_ij) = 1 → L = (Paulis commuting with all of T) \ S, i.e. at least one of the two parities odd; reason: with both parities odd any two elements of L commute (sign |R₁||C₂| + |R₂||C₁| ≡ 1 + 1 ≡ 0), so S ∪ L would be abelian, contradicting "This combined group is not Abelian"; counterexample n = 3, L₁ = (X-row 0)(Z-column 0), L₂ = (X-row 0)(Z-column 1) both satisfy (13) and commute; the printed L also excludes X̄ of (25)
-- source:
--   D. Bacon, Operator Quantum Error Correcting Subsystems for Self-Correcting Quantum Memories, arXiv:quant-ph/0506023v4, §IV.A, eq. (13) and p.5 after (13)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_InT
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_symp
import Definitions.Def_BaconOQEC_SGroup

namespace BaconOQEC

/-- Bacon, *Operator Quantum Error Correcting Subsystems for Self-Correcting Quantum Memories*,
arXiv:quant-ph/0506023v4, §IV.A, eq. (13) and p.5 after (13) (corrected): "The final set of
operators which we will consider, L, is similar to S except that the evenness condition becomes
an oddness condition … This set does not by itself form a group, but together with S it does form
a group. This combined group is not Abelian. L has the property that all of its elements commute
with those of T and S." With `C` the Pauli operators commuting with all of `T` and `L := C \ S`:
1. `C` is exactly the set of `P(a, b)` whose `a` is constant along every row and whose `b` is
   constant along every column (products of full `X`-rows and full `Z`-columns);
2. `C` is a group (contains `0`, closed under products);
3. `S ∪ L = C` (so `S` together with `L` forms a group) while `L` alone does not contain the identity;
4. `C` is not abelian;
5. every element of `L` commutes with every element of `T` and of `S`.

**Formalization Note.** Indices are 0-based (`Fin (m + 1)`, lattice size `n = m + 1`, so `n ≥ 1`); Pauli operators
modulo phase are pairs `(a, b)` of bit matrices; commutation is `symp = 0`. Correction: the printed (13) requires
both parities odd; then any two elements of `L` commute (sign `|R₁||C₂| + |R₂||C₁| ≡ 0`) and `S ∪ L`
would be abelian, contrary to the page, and `L` would exclude `X̄` of (25). Here `L` is the
centralizer of `T` minus `S` (at least one parity odd). -/
theorem centralizer_T (m : ℕ) :
    let C : Set (Bits m × Bits m) := {v | ∀ w : Bits m × Bits m, InT w.1 w.2 → symp v w = 0}
    let L : Set (Bits m × Bits m) := C \ (SGroup m : Set (Bits m × Bits m))
    (∀ v : Bits m × Bits m,
      v ∈ C ↔ (∀ i j j', v.1 i j = v.1 i j') ∧ (∀ i i' j, v.2 i j = v.2 i' j)) ∧
    (0 : Bits m × Bits m) ∈ C ∧ (∀ v ∈ C, ∀ w ∈ C, v + w ∈ C) ∧
    (SGroup m : Set (Bits m × Bits m)) ∪ L = C ∧ (0 : Bits m × Bits m) ∉ L ∧
    (∃ v ∈ C, ∃ w ∈ C, symp v w = 1) ∧
    (∀ v ∈ L, (∀ w : Bits m × Bits m, InT w.1 w.2 → symp v w = 0) ∧
      ∀ w ∈ SGroup m, symp v w = 0) := by sorry

end BaconOQEC
